findings.evidence:
Page 2 is only `select {
case v := <-ch: use(v) // had one
default: // nothing buffered yet
}` with the note "default makes select never block", while the definition — "Every case must be a channel operation", "It proceeds with whichever op is ready first" — does not appear until page 8 ("select: Multiplexing"). The identical snippet is repeated at the end of page 7 under "default Is Non-Blocking".

"select {
case v, ok := <-a: use(v, ok) // spins
case v, ok := <-b: use(v, ok)
}" paired with the claim "After `a` closes, its case fires on every iteration", and the fix "if !ok { a = nil; continue } // drop out" — no `for` appears in either snippet.
